{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on F's failed focused run 0dd3281a: run the same body once more\nnow, at the same head 9578637f with the same pins.\n\nReason. The run ended in a native crash of Node after 12 seconds, with\nno assertion failure. A source or test change cannot answer a crash\nthat no case reported. One more run of 12 seconds is the cheapest\nexperiment, and it is a control, not a sampling retry: it tells a\ncrash that repeats from one that does not. Keep the failed history as\nit stands, and record the second run as its own history.\n\nThen:\n- If the second run passes, it is the focused result for F. Name the\n  crash and its diagnostic in the implementation note as a known red,\n  with the control beside it. Continue to the prechecks and the gate.\n- If the second run crashes the same way, the crash repeats and you\n  have a reproduction. Diagnose it in a lane that needs no slot. Name\n  the Node version and the test file that the worker ran.\n- If the second run fails a different way, report it and stop.\n\nThe slot does not wait for the diagnosis. While F has no changed input\nand no passing control, the slot goes to the next ready head in the\norder of lanes act 6fa0190d: H, identifiers (b), JP1, OPS-1, OPS-2.\nG item 1 still waits for F. F takes the slot again as soon as it has a\npassing control or a fix.\n\nI checked the machine at 16:29 EST: 1.2 TiB of disk free, 83 percent of\nmemory free, 0.25 MiB of swap in use, load 2.9. Those are observations\nafter the crash, as yours are.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}